WHAT ARE KEYS IN SQL?
---------------------
Keys are used to identify rows uniquely and establish relationships between tables.

Keys help in:
  • Uniquely identifying records
  • Preventing duplicate data
  • Creating relationships between tables
  • Maintaining data integrity

Simple Definition:
  Keys are columns used to identify records uniquely.

================================================================================
TYPES OF KEYS IN SQL
================================================================================

Main types of keys:
  1. Primary Key
  2. Foreign Key
  3. Candidate Key
  4. Super Key
  5. Alternate Key
  6. Composite Key

================================================================================
EXAMPLE TABLE: STUDENTS
================================================================================

+------------+---------------------------------------+-------+--------+
| student_id | email                                 | phone | name   |
+------------+---------------------------------------+-------+--------+
| 101        | ravi@gmail.com                        | 999111| Ravi   |
| 102        | priya@gmail.com                       | 999222| Priya  |
+------------+---------------------------------------+-------+--------+

================================================================================
1. PRIMARY KEY
================================================================================

Definition: A Primary Key uniquely identifies each row in a table.

Characteristics:
  • Unique values
  • Cannot contain NULL
  • Only one primary key per table

Syntax:
  CREATE TABLE Students
  (
      student_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
      name VARCHAR(50)
  );

Example:
+------------+-------+
| student_id | name  |
+------------+-------+
| 101        | Ravi  |
| 102        | Priya |
+------------+-------+

Why Primary Key? student_id uniquely identifies every student.

Primary Key Diagram:
  Table Rows
       ↓
  Primary Key
       ↓
  Unique Identification

Primary Key Execution (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Insert row.
  Step 2: Database checks primary key.
  Step 3: Verify uniqueness.
  Step 4: Insert allowed if unique.

Duplicate Example:
  INSERT INTO Students VALUES(101, 'Arun');

Result: ERROR - Duplicate Primary Key

================================================================================
2. FOREIGN KEY
================================================================================

Definition: A Foreign Key creates relationship between two tables.
It references the primary key of another table.

Example Tables:

Departments:
+---------+-----------+
| dept_id | dept_name |
+---------+-----------+
| 1       | HR        |
| 2       | IT        |
+---------+-----------+

Employees:
+--------+----------+---------+
| emp_id | emp_name | dept_id |
+--------+----------+---------+
| 101    | Ravi     | 1       |
| 102    | Priya    | 2       |
+--------+----------+---------+

Syntax:
  CREATE TABLE Employees
  (
      emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
      emp_name VARCHAR(50),
      dept_id INT,
      FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id)
  );

Meaning: dept_id in Employees refers to Departments table.

Foreign Key Diagram:
  Departments Table (Primary Key dept_id)
              ↓
  Employees Table (Foreign Key dept_id)

Foreign Key Execution (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Insert employee row.
  Step 2: Database checks dept_id.
  Step 3: Verify value exists in parent table.
  Step 4: Insert succeeds if match exists.

Invalid Foreign Key Example:
  INSERT INTO Employees VALUES(103, 'Kiran', 5);

Result: ERROR - Foreign Key Constraint Failed

================================================================================
3. CANDIDATE KEY
================================================================================

Definition: A Candidate Key is a column that can become primary key.

Example:
+------------+-----------------------------------+
| student_id | email                             |
+------------+-----------------------------------+
| 101        | ravi@gmail.com                    |
| 102        | priya@gmail.com                   |
+------------+-----------------------------------+

Both are unique. Possible candidate keys: student_id, email.

Candidate Key Diagram:
  Multiple Unique Columns
          ↓
  Possible Primary Keys

================================================================================
4. SUPER KEY
================================================================================

Definition: A Super Key is any combination of columns that uniquely identifies rows.

Example possible super keys:
  student_id
  email
  student_id + name

Important Point:
  All candidate keys are super keys.
  But not all super keys are candidate keys.

Super Key Diagram:
  Super Key
       ↓
  Can Identify Rows Uniquely

================================================================================
5. ALTERNATE KEY
================================================================================

Definition: Candidate keys not selected as primary key are called Alternate Keys.

Example:
  If student_id → Primary Key
  Then email → Alternate Key

Diagram:
  Candidate Keys
       ↓
  One Selected → Primary Key
  Others → Alternate Keys

================================================================================
6. COMPOSITE KEY
================================================================================

Definition: A Composite Key uses multiple columns together to uniquely identify rows.

Example Table: StudentCourses
+------------+-----------+
| student_id | course_id |
+------------+-----------+
| 101        | C1        |
| 101        | C2        |
+------------+-----------+

Syntax:
  CREATE TABLE StudentCourses
  (
      student_id INT,
      course_id VARCHAR(10),
      PRIMARY KEY(student_id, course_id)
  );

Meaning: Combination of columns becomes key.

Composite Key Diagram:
  student_id + course_id
          ↓
  Unique Row Identification

================================================================================
DIFFERENCES BETWEEN KEYS
================================================================================

Primary Key vs Foreign Key:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Primary Key              | Foreign Key              |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Uniquely identifies row  | Creates relationship     |
| Cannot contain NULL      | Can contain NULL         |
| One per table            | Multiple allowed         |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

Candidate Key vs Primary Key:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Candidate Key            | Primary Key              |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Possible key             | Selected key             |
| Multiple allowed         | Only one                 |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

Super Key vs Candidate Key:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Super Key                | Candidate Key            |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| May contain extra columns| Minimal unique columns   |
| Not minimal              | Minimal                  |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
SQL EXECUTION EXAMPLE
================================================================================

  CREATE TABLE Departments
  (
      dept_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
      dept_name VARCHAR(50)
  );

  CREATE TABLE Employees
  (
      emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
      emp_name VARCHAR(50),
      dept_id INT,
      FOREIGN KEY(dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id)
  );

Execution Flow:
  Step 1: Create parent table (Departments).
  Step 2: Create child table (Employees).
  Step 3: Define relationships.
  Step 4: Database enforces constraints.

================================================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE – COLLEGE DATABASE
================================================================================

Students Table: Primary Key = student_id
Courses Table:  Primary Key = course_id
Enrollments Table: Composite Key = student_id + course_id

Keys Relationship Diagram:
  Primary Key
        ↓
  Referenced By
        ↓
  Foreign Key

================================================================================
ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES
================================================================================

Advantages:
  1. Prevent duplicate records
  2. Maintain integrity
  3. Improve relationships
  4. Support normalization

Disadvantages:
  1. Too many keys may slow inserts
  2. Complex relationships increase maintenance

